# SYNOPSIS

```usage
svcd
```

# DESCRIPTION

`svcd` is the first process: the kernel starts it as the root task, task 1, from the boot module of that name, unless the kernel command line's `vx.root=<name>` names another.
It takes no arguments.
Its spawn message holds what the kernel gives no one else: a handle to itself, the boot image, the store image on an install medium, the ACPI tables, the root Resource, the kernel command line and the bootloader's entropy.

It reads the service manifests (see svc(6)) under `boot/svc/` straight from the boot image, because bootfs(4), which serves that image as files, is one of the services it starts.
It starts each service the manifests describe, giving it what its manifest names and nothing more, and waits for them to exit.
It serves no files and never exits.

It starts the drivers first, the services whose manifests name device registers or interrupts, so the console is there for the rest; then the other services.
Each group starts in the boot image's order: the manifests of `boot/svc` by file name, then those a test image adds, each manifest's services in the order written.
Once a service has posted `/srv/cons`, `svcd` writes its own messages there too.

@node=manifest title="Manifests"

A manifest is ndb records: a `service=` record, then the records that belong to it, up to the next `service=`.
svc(6) defines the keys; this is what `svcd` does with them.

- A manifest with an ndb error anywhere, or a key svc(6) does not define, is used not at all, rather than with a service cut short at the typo.
- A service whose `arch=` names the other architecture is left out, as is one the command line's `vx.skip=<name>,...` names, or one whose `when=<word>` is not a word of the command line (`vx.live` on an install medium, `vx.system` on an installed system).
- `post=<srv>` makes a post: a channel whose client end `svcd` keeps as `/srv/<srv>` and whose server end the service gets as `listen`.
  `svcd` keeps a second handle to the server end, so a connection made while the service is restarting waits for it.
  A post is a rendezvous: whichever manifest names it first, by `post=`, `claim=` or `connect=`, makes it, and connections wait for a server.
- `claim=<srv>` gives the service the post's server end, to hand on, as devmgr(8) hands `/srv/disk0` to the driver it starts; `connect=<srv>` gives it a connector to the post.
- `mount=` and `bind=` records, and the lines of a namespace template `ns=<name>` (`/lib/ns/<name>` in the boot image, a namespace(6) file), become the mounts and binds of the service's namespace, which vx-rt replays before `vx_main` runs.
  A mount names a post, not a path: `mount=/ srv=bootfs` mounts `/srv/bootfs` on `/`.
- `ioport=`, `mmio=` and `irq=` records make a service a driver: `svcd` mints the device objects they name from the root Resource once, when it reads the manifest, keeps them, and gives each start of the driver its own duplicates, so a restarted driver gets the same device.
- `arg=` and `env=` records become its arguments and environment, in order; `part=` records and the device records are passed on as they are, for partd(8) and the driver; `user=<name>` is who it runs as, `none` without it.
- `restart`: when the service exits, for any reason, `svcd` starts it again, unless it has exited more than five times within ten seconds of the first of those exits; then it says so and leaves it.

Each service runs in a session and note group of its own, as Plan 9's daemons do, so a note to one group never reaches the rest of the system.
`svcd` registers each with procfs(4) before it runs; what it started before procfs it registers once procfs serves, and again whenever procfs restarts.

@node=given title="What a service is given"

A service's spawn message holds these, each only when its manifest asks:

| Flag | Handle | What it is |
| `bootimage` | `bootimage` | the boot image, read-only, with its size |
| `storeimage` | `storeimage` | the store image, read-only, on an install medium |
| `post=` | `listen` | the server end of its post |
| `console` | `console` | a connector to `/srv/cons` |
| `resource` | `resource` | the root Resource: authority over every device (devmgr) |
| `pager` | `pager` | the root Resource with only the right to make pagers (fsd(4)) |
| `acpi` | `acpi` | the ACPI tables, with their size |
| `tasks` | `tasks` | `svcd`'s own task, and through it every task (procfs) |
| `cmdline` | | the kernel command line, as a record |
| `entropy` | | a 32-byte seed of its own, from `svcd`'s generator |
| `claim=` | `claim:<srv>` | a post's server end |
| `connect=` | `srv:<srv>` | a connector to a post |
| `mount=` | `ns.<nn>` | a connector to the mounted post |

and a driver gets `ioport`, `mmio` and `irq` handles for its device records.

# EXAMPLES

The network service, from `boot/svc/netd.ndb`:

```ndb
service=netd program=/boot/bin/netd post=net console restart
connect=ether0
```

The console, a driver with its device minted by `svcd`, on x86_64:

```ndb
service=uart program=/boot/bin/drv-uart-16550 arch=x86_64 post=cons restart
ioport=0x3f8 count=8
irq=4
```

The start of a boot:

```text
svcd: hello from user space (task 1)
svcd: started uart (task 2)
svcd: started bootfs (task 3)
svcd: started devmgr (task 4)
svcd: started netd (task 5)
svcd: started nsd (task 6)
```

# FILES

: `/boot/svc/*.ndb`
  The service manifests, read from the boot image.
: `/lib/ns/<name>`
  The namespace templates `ns=` names, read from the boot image.
: `/srv/<srv>`
  The posts, as mounts and connectors name them.

