# DIAGNOSTICS

`svcd` says what it does on the console: `svcd: started <name> (task <n>)`, and `svcd: <name> exited` followed by the exit string when there is one.
A restarted service's new start is said before its exit, since the service may be the console.
`svcd: <name> keeps exiting; it is not restarted again` ends its restarts.

A manifest with an error is reported with the error and `so none of it is used`; a service with no name or program, or past the 32nd, is skipped; one whose post or device cannot be made is reported and never started.
`cannot start <name>` and `cannot restart <name>` give the error, as does `no program <path> in the boot image`.

When the boot image is missing, cannot be mapped or is malformed, `svcd` prints `svcd: FAILED:` and the reason, and waits forever: the root task never exits.

# BUGS

It holds 32 services and 32 posts, and a post's name is at most 31 bytes.

`/srv` is not a file tree: posts come only from manifests, `ls /srv` fails, and a program cannot post a service at run time.

Namespace templates are applied only by `svcd`, and only their `mount /srv/<name>` and `bind` lines; any other line stops the service from starting.

A service is restarted whatever its exit string, success included, and `svcd` never stops one: there is no orderly shutdown, and no ordering beyond the manifests' (nothing waits for the wall clock, or for a post to be served).

A restarted procfs has lost its table: `svcd` registers its services again, and every other process is gone from `/proc`.

Kernel messages after the console hand-off reach nowhere unless `vx.kconsole` keeps them on the serial port, so a fault in a service shows only as its exit string, such as `sys: trap: fault read addr=… pc=…`.

Entropy is the bootloader's alone, seeded once; a service gets it only if its manifest says `entropy`.
